{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AGENTS.md docs/planning/chunks/C48.md docs/planning/refinements/2026-11-04/plan.md docs/planning/refinements/2026-11-04/verification.md docs/planning/verification-policy.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prisma AIRS Desktop agent guidance\n\n## Current work\n\nThe owner confirmed the 2026-11-04 refinement plan: see\n[the accepted scope](docs/planning/refinements/2026-11-04/plan.md),\n[verification plan](docs/planning/refinements/2026-11-04/verification.md) and\n[execution status](docs/planning/refinements/2026-11-04/execution.md).\nP11 comprises C46 connection/diagnostics, C47 shell, C48 API Client and C49\nSettings/themes. Use focused checks and the affected native flows. Historical\nP09/P10 outcomes, sealed evidence and the published v1.0.0-rc.1 stay unchanged.\nThe RC remains a prerelease; full installed live acceptance is deferred by the\nowner. No new live API calls are authorized by this refinement task.\n\n## Execution location — owner correction, 2026-11-04\n\nInteractive automated development, local builds, focused checks and native UI tests run inside the\ndedicated Worf macOS VM, reachable as `admin@worf-vm` (formerly `airs-vm`). The\ncurrent isolated checkout is\n`/Users/cdot/development/redtail/p09-queue/refinement-20261104/core`, on\n`refinement/20261104-vm`. Preserve the other VM checkouts and their user changes.\nThe separately approved CI/release builder roles remain unchanged: Enterprise\nfor Linux AMD64 and Jadzia initially for macOS ARM64, as in the build/release plan.\n\nKirk is for the owner's live acceptance. Do not drive its desktop or start native\ndevelopment/test launchers there unless the owner explicitly changes this rule.\nThe former Kirk P11 launcher and supervisor are retired. SSH/SCM transport through\nKirk, when needed for the VM's private Git service route, performs no application\nbuilds, tests or UI actions.\n\nUse the VM's pinned tools and its granted `agent-desktop` for owned-window\nactivation. Bind every native action to the exact fixture bundle, PID and process\nlifetime. Existing source checks executed on Kirk before this correction retain\ntheir actual original provenance; do not relabel them VM runs or replay unrelated\nsuites merely to refresh their source fingerprint.\n\n## Historical baseline\n\nP09 attempt-02 passed actual independent9/9/9/9/9 and its audit on2026-11-02.\nC31attempt07 andC32attempt23 passed normally at all9; C30/C41 retain explicit owner\nstanding with raw historical scores unchanged. C45/P10 is the approved current\nimplementation: one presentation, Prisma AIRS Desktop1.0.0, new io.cdot.airs-desktop\nidentities and fresh setup without alpha/v2 data or credential reads/migration.\nC26/P08 remains open. Later owner authorization produced the signed/notarized\nv1.0.0-rc.1 prerelease; do not interpret that as stable acceptance.\n\n## Verification policy for C45/P10 and future work\n\nUse [verification-policy.md](docs/planning/verification-policy.md). Plan checks\nfrom changed behavior/dependencies/boundary risk. C45 warrants ONE broad current\nidentity/isolation/cleanup baseline after focused preflight/cross-consumer review.\nFollow-up fixes/merges and P10 use affected checks and explicit exact-artifact or\nunchanged-impact evidence carry; a commit, worktree, review, score or source hash\nalone never triggers another full native/Rust suite. Original raw results and\nartifact bindings remain unchanged. Every required assertion/check is accounted.\nHistorical P09 verifiers/results remain untouched. Actual independent chunk/phase\nreview and release requirements remain in force; this policy grants no live calls.\n\nProduct endpoints are called only within explicit controlled live scope. L19’s\noriginal two plus separately approved third reads are all charged; no additional\nL19 live call is authorized. The next fixture gates need no real credentials.\nDo not copy host Keychains, private manifests, CLI tenant TOMLs or auth profiles.\n\n## Accepted direction\n\n- React presentation hosted by Tauri; focused Rust application implementation.\n- Use the Rust SCM SDK directly; no shipped Node/Bun backend runtime or\n  TypeScript backend workers. Build tooling and React WebView JavaScript are\n  distinct from a shipped backend runtime.\n- Academy owns its learner presentation and hosted learning contracts. Share its\n  React components/themes/shell where suitable; preserve hosted grading/access\n  authority. Academy gamification stays in the Academy feature.\n- Selectively evaluate cdot-framework modules and architecture patterns. Do not\n  carry media generation, commercial billing, MySQL/Redis, or its server stack\n  into the desktop runtime. Preserve upstream provenance/licenses.\n- Cover working v1 behavior in testable slices; classify previews/stubs explicitly.\n- macOS Apple Silicon first; v2 coexists with v1 using independent application,\n  protected-storage, and local-data identities.\n- Rust owns credential access, tenant context, request authority, and reviewed\n  configuration changes. Harness proposals do not authorize Apply.\n- D060 removes alpha import from the one presentation. C45/P10 removes the\n  remaining alpha readers/contracts and starts the new namespace with fresh\n  setup; no alpha/v2 data, credentials or approvals are migrated.\n- Offline local work is supported; no silent replay of remote operations.\n- Remote Gateway usage/cost/budget/limit administration is product functionality,\n  not desktop application monetization.\n\n## Source ownership and working safety\n\n- This repository: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.\n- Framework reference: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/ampersand-services`.\n- V1 behavioral reference: `/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-desktop`.\n- Shared Academy: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/ai-security-academy`.\n- SDK: `airs.coder:/home/cdot/development/cdot65/scm-sdk-rs`.\n- Desktop-capable native Harness source:\n  `/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-harness-desktop-mcp`.\n\nRead applicable guidance before changing another checkout. Preserve active SDK\nwork and user changes. Pin accepted external revisions for reproducible inputs;\ndo not assume evolving worktrees or an older similarly named Harness checkout\nmatch the required contracts. Current research evidence is a snapshot, not live\nqualification. Never read or log credentials for planning.\n\n## Reviews and completion\n\nMost original implementation was Claude Code; the owner authorized Codex for\nthe current integration and remediation. Under D026, every implementation chunk and every phase\n(cumulatively) requires a real independent Codex review scoring at least 9/10 in\narchitecture, behavior, authority_security, validation and overall, with empty\nrequired_fixes, blockers and evidence_gaps. Historical G00/G01 Claude reviews\nare retained unchanged and cover only their recorded inputs.\nUnder D034 (superseding D032 for chunks) a chunk completes on a review scoring\nat least 8 in all five dimensions with no blockers (`xtask complete\n--owner-accept REASON`); the ledger records the acceptance and every accepted\nfailure (required fixes, evidence gaps). Phase reviews still need 9/10 with no\nowner acceptance, and every chunk of the phase accepted below 9 must first be\nremediated and re-reviewed to a passing 9/10 (a new generation, later chunks\nnot reopened); `xtask complete` enforces both.\nRetain source/dependency fingerprints, raw verdicts, commands, test results, and\nremediation attempts. Re-review changed source. Never fabricate a score, substitute\nself-review, edit a verdict, or treat favorable scores as evidence that unrun tests\npassed. A queue acknowledgement or process exit status is not a verdict.\n\nFollow `docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`, the ordered C01–C26 instructions,\nreview/test/dependency/release contracts and canonical prompts. G01 specifies\nconcrete interfaces and exact native/check commands before implementation.\nDo not invent these future gates as completed results.\nKeep sensitive raw evidence private and repository records redacted.\n\nThe owner specified `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git` as\nthis repository's destination. Use normal pushes only when publication is within\nthe active task; do not force-push, rewrite unrelated repositories, promote v2\nover v1, or publish application releases during planning.\n# C48 — API Client refinement\n\nPhase P11; owner-approved D063. Covers F06–F11.\n\nUse the [accepted plan](../refinements/2026-11-04/plan.md) and\n[verification plan](../refinements/2026-11-04/verification.md). The four bounded\nstreams may be implemented in parallel and qualified on one integrated fixture\ncandidate after focused preflight. Runtime authority remains in Rust.\n\nThe exact focused command plan is registered in checks.json. Native assertions\nare C48-N01–N03 in native-checks.json; execute the affected flows on the real\nfixture candidate and retain raw outcomes/artifact and driver bindings. Historical\nP09/P10 and RC1 stay immutable. Unchanged baseline evidence retains its original\nbinding and explicit impact rationale; it is not a newly executed pass. A commit,\nmerge or review alone never requests the full native/Rust matrix.\n\nNo real tenant/credential access, live calls or automatic remote configuration\nchanges are authorized. Synthetic fixtures are sufficient for local proof; actual\nremote warning causes remain unknown absent separately designated live scope.\n\nRequire a real independent D034 chunk review, report its actual five scores and\nretain every finding. P11 requires actual 9/9/9/9/9 with no required fixes, blockers\nor evidence gaps. A favorable source review is not native/build qualification.\n**Prisma AIRS Desktop refinement plan — accepted**\n\nOwner feedback of 2026-11-04, all 17 items and Q1–Q13 decisions. The owner confirmed the complete draft with “confirmed. proceed.” Implementation is authorized. This is the accepted scope; completed checks and remaining qualification are recorded separately in execution.md.\n\nThe source assessment is bound to accepted Forgejo main f1e58da5cdeb1c67eed650b186081661cefc2fa2 and Linux platform branch 0178b25a577f67a98152bbf3e3cfc0ab544d888b. The published Mac RC1 remains immutable and stable acceptance remains deferred. Common refinements start from accepted main and are integrated into the Linux workstream without bringing unfinished Linux enablement into the Mac release.\n\n**Agreed behavior**\n\n- Every new tenant, including first-run, Add and a replacement tenant, needs a TSG ID and successful management OAuth authentication before it is committed. Scan Key is separate and optional during setup; scans remain unavailable until a key is configured. Configuration does not initiate a validation scan; the scan service checks the credential when the user actually submits a scan.\n- Existing tenants continue to load. Expired or session-only credentials do not restart onboarding. Product-specific authorization failures remain distinct from authentication failures.\n- Explain is a right overlay drawer that does not shrink the page. The page remains interactive and navigation can continue. The drawer has its own scrolling, Close and scoped Escape behavior; existing dialogs retain priority. Tenant switching closes it and retires the previous tenant's selection.\n- Appearance remains Match system / Light / Dark. Theme independently selects Prisma AIRS or Gruvbox, with light and dark variants. Prisma AIRS is the default. Existing appearance preferences are retained.\n- Desktop themes cover desktop shell/product pages. Academy retains its current general light/dark mapping and Academy-controlled cosmetic rules. Desktop selection does not grant earned cosmetics.\n- SCM is the initial collection when no selection preference exists. Later built-in/custom choices persist. Explicitly closing a collection persists a closed state. Missing or moved custom folders produce recovery choices instead of silent replacement or folder creation.\n\n**Four implementation workstreams**\n\n| Workstream | Feedback coverage | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tenant connection and setup diagnostics | F01, F02, authentication/editing parts of F16 | Reliable authentication gate, precise current setup checks, correct Supply Chain routing and safe tenant lifecycle. |\n| Shell hierarchy and Explain | F03, F04, F05, F17 | One page title in the app header, compact responsive description/actions, overlay Explain, no redundant Ask Harness, improved sun icon. |\n| API Client | F06–F11 | Colored JSON, one URL field, scoped footer metrics, sensible export names, scrolling tabs and remembered collections. |\n| Settings and themes | F12–F16 | Compact cards, separate appearance/theme controls and clear tenant configuration. |\n\n**1. Tenant connection and setup diagnostics**\n\nUse the existing isolated setup draft/context for all new-tenant entry points. Settings currently has a credential-free CreateTenant path; it must not bypass the new rule or configure credentials against the previous active tenant. Rust completion must enforce authentication itself. Bind authentication evidence to the current draft, client, TSG and credential generation, invalidating it on changes. Cancel, retry, late replies and failed persistence must preserve the previous active tenant and clean up abandoned draft credentials.\n\nCurrent ManagementBinding.verify obtains/uses OAuth and then reads one Security Profiles entry. This conflates authentication with Runtime Security permission. Provide a distinct authentication result and keep per-product capability checks separate. Neither a stored secret reference nor a generic HTTP 403 proves successful authentication.\n\nAdd Recheck setup for an existing tenant. Recheck the relevant finding and resolve its active Inbox warning only after a matching fresh check succeeds. Retain the original dated outcome for diagnostics. Show the exact failing source and reason instead of only “2 of 3.” Preserve source/result identity across tenant, workspace and credential changes. Genuine configuration and permission failures remain visible with a precise action.\n\nThe Gateway warning compares IAM resources with the selected workspace slug while displaying its UUID. The real remote cause is still unknown. Preserve the documented slug contract until evidence establishes a defect; do not automatically alter remote IAM configuration.\n\nRoute Model/Skill findings to the modelSkill administration section, carrying the product through the complete Inbox arrival path. Use AI Supply Chain Security as the product display name, retaining Model scans and Skill scans as subordinate capabilities. Home Refresh and Inbox Read now have the same readTenant scope; label both Refresh. Ordinary Refresh must not imply that the setup IAM check was rerun.\n\n**2. Shell hierarchy and Explain**\n\nRemove the header Ask Harness navigation button. Keep the sidebar destination. Move the page title into a real, focusable app-header H1 and retain navigation focus and main-region labeling. Academy course/unit headings and breadcrumbs remain intact.\n\nEach content page starts with a concise description beside its relevant controls: Home refresh, Inbox refresh, Academy account badge, Test Cases count/actions, API collection/environment selectors and Administration expand/collapse. Give actions sufficient space and bound the description width/length. Allow wrapping at narrow effective widths and zoom; controls and complete accessible text remain reachable.\n\nReuse call history, selection and redaction logic inside the new nonmodal Explain drawer. Define Close/Escape ownership, return focus where appropriate, retain dialog priority and clear previous-tenant selection. Replace the gear-like sun artwork while preserving the existing switch-to-light action and accessible semantics.\n\nRetain the Rust catalogue and existing authority checks, and preserve exactly-once Save behavior when changing the editors. The previously discussed platform-hint and Linux tenant-shortcut cleanup remains a separate follow-up outside these 17 items. Craft-keymap, remapping, profiles and shortcut imports remain deferred.\n\n**3. API Client**\n\nColor JSON keys/values in the request JSON editor and Pretty response presentation. Keep request text, raw responses and saved/copied bytes authoritative. In-progress invalid JSON remains editable; invalid/truncated response previews degrade gracefully. Preserve current body/preview limits, read-only states and draft protection. Render numeric/string tokens accurately, including large integers and escapes. Reuse the existing editor foundations with only the required JSON support; no general new UI framework.\n\nReplace the external base-variable badge plus full URL input with one editable URL template and inline variable coloring. Preserve the string-valued URL contract, selection/cursor behavior and complete-template copy/paste. This repairs presentation without rewriting stored collection URLs.\n\nMove request timing/size/status information into the existing bottom status bar beside Ready and the collection request count. Scope it to the displayed response and its run/request/history identity. Retain a clear history identity when a historical response is selected, and prevent late/background responses from replacing another tab/collection's status. Preserve every active transfer's Cancel control and keep save-state messages distinct from response metrics.\n\nFor automatic Save Response suggestions, turn whitespace runs into single hyphens: “My request” becomes “My-request-response.json.” Preserve case, extension and the response suffix. Respect a filename and directory explicitly chosen in the native picker; exported response bytes remain exact.\n\nUse one horizontally scrolling request-tab row, keeping tabs readable and revealing selected/focused tabs. Preserve selection, closing and keyboard behavior.\n\nPersist a collection selection with distinct uninitialized, built-in/custom and explicitly closed states. Apply startup selection once through the core load path. Preserve SCM's edit-aware refresh, original collections and credentials. Missing/replaced custom folders keep their preference and offer Retry, Open collection and SCM recovery actions.\n\n**4. Settings and themes**\n\nUse compact responsive cards and appropriate controls instead of full-width form rows:\n\n- Appearance: Appearance and Theme dropdowns.\n- Academy: account/settings card, retaining the existing learner flow.\n- Diagnostics: existing export/diagnostic actions, including dated warning results.\n- Tenant section: separate Scan Key, Management Client, Scan Destination, Update Tenant and Add Tenant cards.\n\nCredential cards retain secure-storage/session-only choices and Replace/Remove actions. Keep secret fields uncontrolled, clear them before asynchronous work, and preserve context-keyed remount/cleanup. Add Tenant includes label, TSG, management client ID/secret and scan-region selection. OAuth authentication is required; Scan Key setup is available separately. Reuse existing activation/switch guards.\n\nUpdate Tenant selects a profile and offers Edit/Delete. Edit initially changes label/environment. TSG identity changes create a replacement tenant. Destination changes retain their own reviewed card, including advanced custom HTTPS support; Add Tenant has the region dropdown instead of region/custom-endpoint radio buttons. Delete retains existing confirmation and cleanup behavior.\n\nProvide light/dark Prisma AIRS and Gruvbox palettes, defaulting to Prisma AIRS. Make theme persistence and existing-state defaults explicit. PersistedState rejects unknown fields, so a new preference must have a deliberate storage/migration and downgrade policy, rather than assuming old binaries can read it. Preserve the new app namespace and never read alpha/v2 data.\n\nIsolate desktop palette styling from Academy, including portaled content. The existing LearnerHost theme prop alone does not isolate the global CSS variables. Check contrast, focus states and shared controls in all four palette/appearance combinations.\n\n**Integration and verification**\n\nAgree shared command/state, authentication and CSS contracts first. Assign ownership for overlapping Settings and shell files, then parallelize the bounded streams. Integrate common changes into the Linux branch while preserving its platform-specific adapters and Harness pin. Save all work on normal remote feature branches; preserve unrelated worktrees and owner changes.\n\nStart each reported defect with a focused local reproducer/fixture at the actual affected interface. Distinguish reproduced behavior from source risks and remote facts. Authentication fixtures cover invalid OAuth versus authenticated-but-forbidden product reads, direct completion bypass attempts, changed credentials, cancellation, failed persistence, late replies and wrong-tenant isolation. Warning fixtures cover matching rechecks, history and the complete Supply Chain navigation path.\n\nRun affected frontend tests/typechecking and targeted Rust checks. Exercise the changed native flows on Mac and Linux using owned fixture state: credential entry/cleanup, focus and nonmodal drawer behavior, 100%/200% layouts, JSON/URL editing/copy, tab scrolling, footer ownership, Save exactly once and Save Response picker behavior. Review themes on representative shell/product/Academy/portal surfaces.\n\nFollow the owner's verification strategy. Preserve original raw outcomes and reuse unrelated evidence with exact-artifact proof or an honest unchanged-impact rationale. A commit, merge or review alone does not request a full Rust suite or 19 native gates. Follow standing independent chunk/phase review rules, report every actual score and verify only affected remediation. Complete focused checks and cross-consumer review before building the combined qualification candidate. Linux's first platform qualification remains a separate requirement.\n\nNo real credentials or product calls are needed for local fixtures. If the actual Gateway/Supply Chain findings still require a live diagnosis, prepare a bounded read-only scope for that tenant/workspace under the existing live-manifest rule. Keep authentication material out of logs and make no automatic remote configuration changes.\n\nPackage accepted changes as a new immutable candidate; preserve existing RC1 assets. Stable promotion still requires acceptance of the exact final artifact. Acceptance of this plan does not claim implementation, qualification or publication has completed.\n# P11 verification plan\n\nThis plan applies D062 to the owner-approved refinements. It does not reopen or\nreinterpret historical P09/P10 results. Select checks from changed behavior,\ndependencies and authority boundaries; no full Rust/native replay per commit.\n\n| Stream | Changed boundaries | Focused proof | Affected native proof |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| C46 connection/diagnostics | OAuth, draft commitment, credential/context retirement, warning persistence and Inbox arrival | Real synthetic TLS issuer vs product permissions; completion bypass, cancellation, persistence and stale replies; equivalent and legacy warning rechecks; strict wire validation | New tenant with required OAuth and optional Scan Key; existing tenant warnings, source reason, recheck and retained diagnostic; correct Supply Chain arrival |\n| C47 shell | Shared page heading/actions, drawer focus and tenant retirement | Header/Explain navigation, pending preference race, modal priority, all PageIntro consumers | Interactive overlay without page shrink; navigation, close/Escape/focus; representative pages at 100%/200%, light/dark and narrow widths |\n| C48 API | Editable token overlays, response/history status ownership, file suggestion, collection startup persistence | Exact strings/large integers/invalid JSON; late cross-tab/collection replies; startup/close/custom-root recovery; picker suggestion vs selected path | JSON/URL input, cursor/selection/copy, Save exactly once, tabs scroll, footer/Cancel ownership, native Save Response picker |\n| C49 Settings/themes | Tenant credential cards, metadata edit, additive storage preference, desktop/Academy CSS | Isolated Add draft, edit/delete target, secret cleanup; old-state defaults and alternate-theme persistence; four-palette contrast and Academy isolation | Cards/controls and keyboard navigation; both themes in light/dark; learner/portal isolation; secure/session credential controls |\n\nUse Rust 1.99.0 and Node 24.21.0 with the locked dependency set. All local network\nfixtures use generated credentials and loopback endpoints. No real tenant or\nKeychain content is inspected. Genuine remote warning causes remain unknown\nuntil separately designated read-only scope exists.\n\nKeep immutable raw red/green outcomes and command/source bindings. A shared Rust\ntarget exposed cross-worktree workspace metadata during fixture migration.\nThose raw infrastructure failures remain recorded. Workspace outputs were removed\nonly from private CoW target copies; public dependency artifacts were retained\nwith a hash/inode check and the shared cache was left untouched. Re-establish\naffected Rust results with private source-bound outputs. Frontend checks are\nunaffected. This is a bounded cache correction, not a full suite rerun trigger.\n\nFinish focused checks and cross-consumer review before building the native\ncandidate. Use one final fixture candidate for affected Mac checks and all P11\nreviews when valid. Test doubles and DOM results do not prove native focus,\nWebKit layout, picker or Keychain behavior. Linux native/platform qualification\nremains separate; an available Linux runner is not product qualification.\n\nUnchanged code paths may retain their original evidence with an explicit impact\nrationale and original artifact binding. Changed bundle bytes cannot claim exact\nartifact equivalence to RC1. Full end-to-end testing is reserved for release\nqualification or an impact that cannot be bounded; neither a new commit nor a\nreview score alone triggers it. Every new assertion is accounted explicitly.\n\nIndependent chunk reviews use actual scores under D034; phase P11 requires 9\nin all dimensions and no required fixes, blockers or evidence gaps. Do not infer\ncompletion from a source test, an agent exit or a queue acknowledgement.\n# Risk-based verification strategy — owner direction, 2026-11-02\n\nStatus: future policy, including C45/P10. Record and implement it before the next phase's qualification. Do not interrupt, change or retroactively reinterpret the active P09 workflow, its sealed inputs, reviews or historical outcomes.\n\nThe owner wants materially faster iteration while preserving confidence in the backend/frontend migration. Full end-to-end qualification is reserved for changes that warrant it. The earlier 2026-11-01 exact-artifact verification-reuse decision remains in force.\n\n## Select verification from the change\n\nBefore expensive work, write a short verification plan: changed behavior and components, authority/data/platform boundaries touched, affected tests/native flows, retained evidence and its justification, uncertainty, and the reason for any full run. Keep this a small record using the existing runner, not a new orchestration framework.\n\n1. During implementation, run meaningful focused unit and production-interface integration regressions, relevant typechecks, and affected lint/format checks. Cover failure ordering, teardown and stale ownership at this layer first. Rust test-only or lint-only changes require their relevant targets, not an unrelated workspace or native suite.\n2. For changed user-facing or native behavior, exercise the affected native flows and boundaries. Include related consumers of shared changed components. Use a small startup/navigation/critical-action smoke set when integration or packaging makes it useful.\n3. Run the full end-to-end matrix when justified by broad architectural/runtime migration; wide native bridge, shared authority or lifecycle changes; application identity, protected storage or install changes; release-candidate qualification; or impact/evidence uncertainty that cannot be bounded confidently.\n4. A new commit, worktree, merge, review attempt, review score, timestamp or source fingerprint does not by itself justify full re-execution. Decide from actual behavior, dependencies, artifact and execution-condition changes.\n\nWhen a bug reveals a shared invariant, inspect all its relevant callers and add cross-consumer production-path regressions before sealing. For example, test native confirmation ownership at admission, queued presentation, shown presentation and retirement across applicable callers together. Catch the class of failure before another long UI run.\n\nValidate native assertion maps and their positive/negative prerequisites cheaply. Loading, errors or incomplete screens cannot prove that a successful detail surface lacks forbidden actions.\n\n## Preserve and reuse evidence honestly\n\n- For exact reuse, verify the actual executable and complete canonical app bundle, relevant per-driver helper/import closure, build flags, and execution conditions against the original passing evidence. Run newly affected checks only. A source-input hash change alone must not force native or Rust suite repetition.\n- When application behavior or relevant artifact bytes change, execute affected checks on the current candidate. Unaffected historical coverage may be carried with an explicit dependency/impact justification and original artifact binding; do not call it a current execution or assert byte equality that does not hold. Broaden testing when that justification is uncertain.\n- For an isolated native driver/helper change, run its coverage validators and affected driver consumers. Retain other valid completed drivers when their own helper closure, app artifact and relevant conditions remain valid.\n- Every required assertion needs accounted coverage. Distinguish executed now, reused with exact proof, carried unaffected with rationale, not applicable with reason, and explicit owner-accepted risk. An assertion cannot disappear through omission or a score change.\n- Preserve raw logs, failures, verdicts, source inventories, hashes and original run records unchanged. New proof references them; it never rewrites their provenance or turns an unperformed check into a pass.\n- Missing or inconsistent proof requires the affected verification. New requirement retirements and accepted risks retain their existing owner-decision rules.\n\n## Coalesce expensive work\n\nFinish focused checks, source/interface review and cross-consumer risk inspection before freezing a candidate. Resolve known lint, typing, contract and fixture/assertion problems before starting the long matrix.\n\nQualify one stable candidate at the justified integration boundary. Let chunk and phase reviews consume that same qualified artifact and evidence when validity is preserved. A formal review still examines the actual current source and evidence and reports its real scores; it does not automatically schedule another full test run.\n\nFor review fixes, execute the causal regression and affected checks, then update the impact/evidence record. Remediation or merge triggers another full run only when its actual risk warrants one. Do not reopen unaffected chunks or repeat unrelated checks merely to refresh source provenance.\n\nKeep long native jobs durable and independent of model service lifetime, with one UI writer, ownership/source guards, per-step immutable results and a resumable queue. Recover from infrastructure failure at the first unfinished valid step. Do not restart completed valid steps.\n\n## Apply concretely to C45/P10\n\nC45's new application/Keychain/data identities, fresh-setup isolation and broad presentation cleanup warrant one complete current cumulative native baseline, including new identity/isolation assertions, after implementation and focused preflight settle. Preserve all P09 evidence.\n\nAfter that baseline, use affected checks for C45 fixes. P10 may consume the same qualified candidate rather than automatically repeating the full C44/C45 matrix and Rust suite. Integration gets checks for actual merge changes; an unchanged qualified artifact and helper closure reuse the existing proof.\n\nAfter P09 has passed, copy this policy into the isolated C45 worktree at docs/planning/verification-policy.md. Update the C45 brief's blanket rerun wording, repository guidance, test catalogue, review protocol and new P10 provenance/gates to implement this owner direction explicitly. Keep historical P09 verifiers and results unchanged. Keep implementation small: a change-impact/verification-plan record and strict validation of selected checks and evidence reuse.\n\nRetain existing chunk/phase independent-review and release requirements, including separately recorded owner exceptions. This strategy changes verification scope and scheduling; it does not fabricate scores, bypass assertion coverage, grant live access, or authorize signing/publication.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
